  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nToday through Thursday:\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon features largely zonal flow \nacross the northern half of the CONUS with a positively-tilted \nshortwave trough extending from the Dakotas/northwest MN southwest \nto a cutoff low over the southern Great Basin. This cutoff \nlow/shortwave and the remnants of the former hurricane Polo remain \nthe focus for the forecast period, with strong moisture \nadvection expected to contribute to efficient rainfall processes\nthat will be present through the first half of Thursday. Hidden\nin higher river/flash flooding threat, severe weather will also\nbe possible Wednesday with a 5% chance of damaging hail/wind or\nan isolated tornado near that reaches from Kansas into far \nsouth-central Nebraska.\n\nTaking a look at the synoptic setup for this period, the remnant MCV \nfrom Polo will join the Great Basin low as they eject to the \nnortheast. This will help initially deepen the mid/upper lows, and \npromote a stronger mass response across the Southern and Central \nPlains that will amp up warm air advection and moisture \ntransport from the Gulf in addition to the remnants from Polo. \nAlready, a cold front stretches from eastern ND to southeast CO,\nwhile a warm front extends from there towards LA. That \naforementioned cold front will shift into the forecast area \nlater this evening, stalling out/becoming increasingly \npositively-tilted, and remaining in the area through the first \nhalf of Thursday. Convergence along the front as moisture \ntransport runs into the frontal interface along with deeper lift\nassociated with the large-scale height falls will help \"skinny\"\nCAPE profiles to be realized and lead to efficient rainfall \nprocesses. \n\nLooking at the periods of peak rainfall, the first wave of healthy \nrainfall is set to occur this overnight tonight into the morning \nhours (4 AM to 10 AM). After a lull in activity, strong convergence \nin the lowest levels will help to spread stronger storm activity \nnorthward from Kansas into southern Nebraska after 3 PM tomorrow. \nWe'll see our best instability of the forecast to go along with\nmodest shear that could organize some of the stronger storms to\nmake severe activity a possibility. That modest shear and \ncurvature in the low-level hodograph will keep all hazards on \nthe table. Those showers and storms present the highest rainfall\npotential as the move across southeast Nebraska into Iowa from \n3 PM to 3 AM, and amounts during this time could exceed 2\" \nalone. Once that period ends, we'll be dealing with exiting \nrainfall across far southeastern Nebraska into southwest Iowa \nthat will leave us high and dry by Thursday night. \n\nMost of southeast Nebraska are set to receive 2-4\" of rainfall,\nwith an narrow band of 5-6\" or more possible where the strong \nstorms track Wednesday evening. This should translate to areas \nof flash flooding, several area streams and rivers hitting \naction stage, and a handful of locations hitting minor flood \nstages south of the Platte River. River levels should peak \nFriday and Saturday according to ensemble river guidance, and \ncould complicate rural travel if conditions do reach minor flood\nstage. \n\nFriday and Beyond:\n\nFriday onward in the forecast will see the area dry out \nconsiderably, much to the joy of those hoping to harvest. West-\nnorthwesterly flow will move into the area and we'll see \ntemperatures hang in the 60s through the remaining forecast that \nwarms a tad bit towards the end of next week. Very few ensemble \nmodels output any QPF for eastern Nebraska and southwest Iowa, \nmaking for a well-timed dry period that will carry us into \nmeteorological fall with excellent weather.",
